README.OSK -- GNU C 2.7.2 for OS-9/68000, as it is kept on this diskWhat this is  Stephan Paschedag's working tree of GNU C 2.7.2 for OS-9/68000, as he  released it with the compilers in CMDS/GCC272 (1995-97).  readme.osk  here is his own account of using and building it; the compilers were  built from a tree like this one with make_all.IT IS NOT THE WHOLE COMPILER, and this is the most important thing inthis file.  The archive does not carry the OS-9 target description --the machine description (md), tm.h, config.h, aux_output.c and theOSK/ directory of generated insn_*.c files the build log names (log,line 1: "-Iconfig -IOSK OSK/insn_config.h") -- and md.r in it is anempty file.  Nor does it carry the C++ front end: CP/ holds threesource files, where GNU C 2.7.2's cp/ has several dozen.  So cc2 andcc2plus cannot be rebuilt from this tree alone.  What it DOES holdis complete enough for the parts written for OS-9: the driver(gcc2osk.c), the preprocessor (cccp.c, cexp.y), the constructorcollector (collect_osk.c), libgcc (libgcc1.c, libgcc2.c, floatlib.c),the build scripts, and every target-independent pass of the compiler.Where it came from  gccsrc272.zip.  The binaries are gcc272.zip; the C++ libraries  libgpp272.zip and the gclib libraries gclib272.zip travelled with it.Terms  The GNU General Public License, version 2 -- Copying, and Copying.lib  for the library parts.  toplev.c, lines 6-9: "GNU CC is free software;  you can redistribute it and/or modify it under the terms of the GNU  General Public License as published by the Free Software Foundation;  either version 2, or (at your option) any later version."  libgcc2.c  carries the usual exception (line 22 on): linking it into a program  does not by itself put the program under the GPL.What was changed, all of it  1. Nothing in any kept file.  The archive was already OS-9 text, CR     line ends and 7-bit ASCII throughout.  There is no ORIG directory     because no file was edited.  2. Left out, 3,416,004 bytes in 77 files, each named here:     - build products, the ROF libraries (18 files, 275,580 bytes):       floatlib000.l floatlib020.l floatlib300.l libgcc000.l libgcc020.l       libgcc1_000.l libgcc1_020.l libgcc1_300.l libgcc2_000.l       libgcc2_020.l libgcc2_300.l libgcc2u_000.l libgcc2u_020.l       libgcc2u_300.l libgcc300.l libgcc_000.l libgcc_020.l libgcc_300.l       Three of them are installed, in GNULIB, as the gcc272 support       library: libgcc000.l, libgcc020.l and libgcc300.l.     - build products, objects and one module (7 files, 65,846 bytes):       genconfig.r, STAGE0/ STAGE1/ STAGE1_G/ STAGE2/genconfig.r, md.r       (empty), CONFIG/OSK/CMDS/move_if_change     - a build log, its date stamps and a tags file (5 files, 238,028       bytes): log make.log make.date CONFIG/OSK/make.date Tags     - generated documentation (7 files, 2,462,761 bytes): gcc.info,       DOC/gcc.info (a second copy), cpp.aux cpp.cps cpp.fns gcc.aux       gcc.cps.  The Texinfo sources they are made from are kept.     - the editor's bookmark files, *.ze (37 files, 2,588 bytes)     - the FSF's order form and service directory, Orders and Service       (280,428 bytes; Service is not 7-bit ASCII)     - ORG/emit_rtl.c (90,773 bytes), damaged in the archive: from byte       54,785 on it is binary garbage.  The other 18 files of ORG/ are       kept.  3. Module names.  The five programs register new module names,     matching their new file names:         gcc2     ->  gcc272         cccp2    ->  gcc272_cccp2         cc2      ->  gcc272_cc2         cc2plus  ->  gcc272_cc2plus         collect  ->  gcc272_collect     GCC2's 2.5.6 already uses gcc2, cccp2, cc2 and collect, and all of     them are sticky: once run they stay resident and answer to their     name.  The new name is appended to the module and M$Name pointed at     it; no instruction moves.  The code is the 1997 build, byte for byte.How it runs here  The driver checks that /dd/CMDS/gcc_cc2 exists and then forks plain  `cc2', so a pass is pointed at its own file with gcc272's -F option,  and GCC2_OPTS carries default options:    GCC2_OPTS="-Fcccp2x[/dd/CMDS/GCC272/gcc272_cccp2]               -Fcc2x[/dd/CMDS/GCC272/gcc272_cc2]               -Fcc2plusx[/dd/CMDS/GCC272/gcc272_cc2plus]               -Fcollectx[/dd/CMDS/GCC272/gcc272_collect]"   (one line)    gcc272 -uwlibs -L/h1/LIB -o hello hello.c  -uwlibs links the reader's own clibn.l with GNULIB/libgcc000.l.  Microware's r68, l68 and del do the rest, from the reader's system.  gcc272_collect uses Microware's csl; the other four use no trap module.